Now, the product names from supermarket
receipts, the logistic regression

4
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:20,396
won every categories, every split,
30 cells out of 30, and it not

5
00:00:20,662 --> 00:00:22,689
the cheapest model in the study.

6
00:00:23,343 --> 00:00:26,675
That turned out to be the
more interesting result.

7
00:00:27,345 --> 00:00:31,724
This talk is about why and about
where that result stopped being

8
00:00:31,785 --> 00:00:35,751
true I am Vladimir Beskorovainyi.

9
00:00:36,507 --> 00:00:42,160
I design AI system for closed networks on
the hardware the organization already has.

10
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:47,766
One thing before the numbers,
every number in this talk comes

11
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:49,617
from public synthetic benchmark.

12
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:51,778
The code is open.

13
00:00:52,144 --> 00:00:54,001
You can run it yourself.

14
00:00:54,699 --> 00:00:58,171
The architecture itself has
been in production since twenty

15
00:00:58,182 --> 00:00:59,943
twenty-one at national scale.

16
00:01:00,605 --> 00:01:05,854
That part is under NDA, so it,
it stays outside the numbers.

17
00:01:07,568 --> 00:01:12,530
The task, a line from a receipt,
has to be assigned to a consumption

18
00:01:12,794 --> 00:01:15,381
category, COICOP six digits.

19
00:01:15,934 --> 00:01:17,808
The text is short and dirty.

20
00:01:18,303 --> 00:01:24,285
Abbreviations, typos, the seller's
internal codes, words stuck together.

21
00:01:25,170 --> 00:01:27,797
No product codes, no retailer categories.

22
00:01:28,612 --> 00:01:30,819
The benchmark is synthetic and public.

23
00:01:31,241 --> 00:01:33,100
Six categories, five splits.

24
00:01:33,551 --> 00:01:36,785
Every model sees exactly the same data.

25
00:01:37,904 --> 00:01:45,920
I report mean F1, and I compare models
in pairs, cell by cell, not by averages.

26
00:01:46,573 --> 00:01:53,515
An average can hide a model that wins
one, two categories and loses on four.

27
00:01:54,964 --> 00:01:57,032
Here is what runs in production.

28
00:01:57,506 --> 00:02:01,392
The input is every cash
register in the country, tens of

29
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:04,076
millions of product lines a day.

30
00:02:04,923 --> 00:02:06,652
Free text, no product codes.

31
00:02:07,473 --> 00:02:09,110
Four design choices.

32
00:02:10,161 --> 00:02:12,236
Rules pick the category.

33
00:02:13,052 --> 00:02:17,156
A small model for each category
only confirms or rejects.

34
00:02:17,882 --> 00:02:24,478
People and the model vote under one rule,
and a category is configuration, not code.

35
00:02:25,261 --> 00:02:28,879
A methodologist adds one, not an engineer.

36
00:02:29,802 --> 00:02:34,833
The deploying organization reports ninety
percent accuracy on the live stream.

37
00:02:35,673 --> 00:02:40,430
That is their figure, and it
measures the whole pipeline.

38
00:02:41,679 --> 00:02:44,866
Different data, different
rules, different measurement.

39
00:02:45,292 --> 00:02:49,814
I am not going to explain the
number with my benchmarked.

40
00:02:51,288 --> 00:02:56,977
The headline number, character
n-gram with logistic regression, mean

41
00:02:57,036 --> 00:03:00,166
F1 naught point nine nine seven.

42
00:03:01,139 --> 00:03:06,587
Thirty-four percent of the test items
also appear in training word for word.

43
00:03:07,418 --> 00:03:14,543
That is realistic for receipts,
but it inflates any score, so I

44
00:03:14,544 --> 00:03:20,229
measured the unseen items separately,
naught point nine nine six.

45
00:03:21,002 --> 00:03:26,773
The character model's result does
not dependent on the overlap.

46
00:03:27,337 --> 00:03:29,674
For the word level models, it does.

47
00:03:31,041 --> 00:03:34,066
Here is everything trained from scratch.

48
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:36,386
Character n-grams on top.

49
00:03:37,221 --> 00:03:39,052
The deep models at the bottom.

50
00:03:39,359 --> 00:03:42,653
Four points between
the best and the worst.

51
00:03:42,983 --> 00:03:43,977
That looks small.

52
00:03:44,649 --> 00:03:46,789
The next slides show why it's not.

53
00:03:48,307 --> 00:03:51,221
Two things in this paper I had to fix.

54
00:03:51,843 --> 00:03:57,680
The first, an earlier version gave
the deep models less training time.

55
00:03:58,387 --> 00:04:02,280
With a fair budget,
they are not the worst.

56
00:04:03,116 --> 00:04:04,855
They're just no better.

57
00:04:05,289 --> 00:04:08,257
The second, my old timing code was wrong.

58
00:04:09,228 --> 00:04:11,345
It made the cheap models look slow.

59
00:04:11,447 --> 00:04:12,981
The real gap is bigger.

60
00:04:13,696 --> 00:04:19,408
Two hundred and twelve times for the CNN,
two hundred and fifty-eight for the LSTM.

61
00:04:19,543 --> 00:04:23,135
Both are written up in
section six points two.

62
00:04:24,626 --> 00:04:28,917
Now, the part that decides
if a system runs at all.

63
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:32,483
Character n-grams are the most accurate.

64
00:04:33,248 --> 00:04:35,722
They are also the slowest.

65
00:04:36,342 --> 00:04:39,121
Forty-one thousand five
hundred items a second.

66
00:04:39,453 --> 00:04:45,812
The simple bag of words does one hundred
and ninety-one thousand three hundred.

67
00:04:46,658 --> 00:04:48,299
Four point six times faster.

68
00:04:49,929 --> 00:04:55,584
three point of F1 behind at
tens of millions of lines a day.

69
00:04:55,949 --> 00:04:57,469
This is not a small thing.

70
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:00,786
It is whether the job finishes.

71
00:05:01,786 --> 00:05:03,456
So there is no single best model.

72
00:05:04,253 --> 00:05:05,982
There are three good choice here.

73
00:05:06,354 --> 00:05:11,843
Pick the one that fits what you
are paid for, accuracy or speed.

74
00:05:12,356 --> 00:05:14,796
The deep models are not on that list.

75
00:05:15,832 --> 00:05:20,008
More than two hundred times the
training cost and no better.

76
00:05:21,351 --> 00:05:22,709
They are not worth.

77
00:05:23,273 --> 00:05:25,363
They just buy you nothing.

78
00:05:26,705 --> 00:05:28,176
What about a transform?

79
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:33,307
I trained DistilBERT on this
task, not point nine nine three.

80
00:05:34,228 --> 00:05:37,880
It is good, but the
character model still wins.

81
00:05:39,048 --> 00:05:41,061
Eighteen cells to the three.

82
00:05:41,699 --> 00:05:46,303
The cost is the problem, eighty-two items
a second against thirty-eight thousand,

83
00:05:46,501 --> 00:05:49,187
about four hundred and sixty times slower.

84
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:53,500
Now the part that limits everything else.

85
00:05:54,243 --> 00:05:57,868
The rules decided which
items reach a model at all.

86
00:05:58,684 --> 00:06:03,685
They let through seventy-five to
eighty-six percent of the right items.

87
00:06:04,142 --> 00:06:09,595
Nothing after that can do better,
and that is the best case.

88
00:06:10,268 --> 00:06:11,779
The rulers stay anyway.

89
00:06:12,262 --> 00:06:18,753
A rule is a phrase a person can read,
change, and defend to an auditor.

90
00:06:19,538 --> 00:06:21,548
A trained model cannot do that.

91
00:06:22,922 --> 00:06:23,944
Now the labels.

92
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:28,106
Each annotator carries a
reliability weight capped at two.

93
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:34,172
In the data, the strong annotators
end at two and so do the weak ones.

94
00:06:34,918 --> 00:06:39,881
The weight saturates, and
it stops telling them apart.

95
00:06:40,515 --> 00:06:42,177
Weighted voting does better.

96
00:06:42,861 --> 00:06:47,314
It buys the classifier one to two points.

97
00:06:48,274 --> 00:06:51,805
Labels are the cheapest
points you can buy.

98
00:06:53,632 --> 00:06:57,644
What this study does not show,
the benchmark is synthetic.

99
00:06:58,146 --> 00:07:00,115
Real receipts are worse.

100
00:07:00,972 --> 00:07:04,573
And here's the number
that keeps me honest.

101
00:07:05,425 --> 00:07:11,693
A published study reported ninety-seven
point four percent on held out test data.

102
00:07:12,686 --> 00:07:18,109
On real unlabeled data, the same
approach coded forty-one point forty

103
00:07:18,143 --> 00:07:21,261
percent of expenditure correctly.

104
00:07:22,003 --> 00:07:26,525
Ninety-seven on a test set,
forty-one in the field.

105
00:07:27,493 --> 00:07:34,211
Every number I showed you today lies
under the warning Four thing to take away.

106
00:07:34,312 --> 00:07:39,846
One, on short, noisy product names, a
character n-gram model was the strongest

107
00:07:39,929 --> 00:07:42,351
thing I measured, not the largest.

108
00:07:43,846 --> 00:07:48,502
Two, put cost in the
same table as accuracy.

109
00:07:49,226 --> 00:07:53,654
Three, fix your labels
before you tune your model.

110
00:07:54,537 --> 00:07:58,596
Four, the architecture matters
more than the model size.

111
00:08:00,343 --> 00:08:02,762
Everything here is, is reproducible.

112
00:08:03,620 --> 00:08:10,019
The preprint is archived to six zero six
point zero two zero zero four, and it is

113
00:08:10,132 --> 00:08:13,205
under review at PeerJ Computer Science.

114
00:08:13,907 --> 00:08:20,747
The code is on GitHub, and the data
and result are on Zenodo with DOI.

115
00:08:21,467 --> 00:08:27,365
Two thing I would like to see done next,
run it on real receipts, not synthetic

116
00:08:27,412 --> 00:08:34,451
ones, and raise the recall of the rule
stage without losing the explainability.

117
